   "Two governments entered the record. The Canadian Centre for Cyber Security's cyber threat bulletin, dated 26 October, says that \"Storm-0940 used an agentic AI framework known as ARTEX against hundreds of organizations\" and that the actor \"moved from selecting a target to gaining privileged access to cloud systems in less than three hours\". The bulletin says \"It is the emergence of a reusable framework that can help conduct cyber operations on an industrial scale\", while also stating that \"Cyber attacks are not yet fully autonomous\" and that \"Decisions that once allowed time for discussion may soon have to be made within minutes\". On 27 October Norway's National Security Authority rated the country's cyber risk as \"heightened\", second on a five-point scale, in what AFP calls NSM's \"first public evaluation of such risks\"; director Arne Christian Haugst\u00f8yl said \"Over the past year, we have observed a tripling of the number of successful cyber operations against Norwegian organisations\", and AFP reports NSM \"did not identify any actors behind the attacks\".",
   "CrowdStrike, publishing on 29 October, says that \"Beginning on October 17, 2026\" its OverWatch team observed a single ARTEX controller used against \"more than 200 distinct organizations worldwide\" in \"at least 14 countries\" over an 11-day window, that it \"confirmed post-exploitation activity at nine organizations\" with the BLOODALCHEMY backdoor, and that the operator ran ARTEX \"primarily with the Chinese-origin DeepSeek LLM, supplemented with a second, subscription-based commercial LLM that CrowdStrike Intelligence could not confirm.\" It says that \"In a single intrusion, VAPOR PANDA's planning agents consumed on the order of 1.4 billion input tokens.\"",
   "CyberScoop, reporting on 30 October, writes that \"Hands-on-keyboard activity tracked by CrowdStrike for the past 15 years typically involved up to 40 commands per hour\", and quotes CrowdStrike's Adam Meyers saying \"Now, with ARTEX, we could see it exceed 1,500 commands an hour.\" CyberScoop reports that CrowdStrike observed roughly 45 agentic adversary attacks in September and 126 for October, and that CrowdStrike \"told CyberScoop about 70% of Vapor Panda's targets were confirmed compromised or had evidence indicating moderate confidence of a successful intrusion\".",
   "Google Threat Intelligence Group wrote on 30 October that \"since late August 2026, we have observed threat actors actively operating popular open-source multi-agent pentesting platforms like ARTEX, PentAGI, CAIRN and other potentially custom harnesses within Mandiant incident response engagements\", calling it \"a paradigm shift towards intrusions occurring at machine speed\". It reports \"individual ARTEX execution sequences ran in compressed bursts of roughly 2 to 25 seconds\", one operation with \"27 events in 23 seconds of active burst time\", and an intrusion that enumerated over 110,000 Active Directory objects and posted output across 491 rotating webhook.site URLs.",
   "Microsoft Threat Intelligence, publishing on 29 October, \"assesses the overall adoption of AI across North Korean threat actors as high\" and says that \"Since late October 2026, Microsoft Threat Intelligence has identified Onyx Sleet and Pearl Sleet leveraging open-source agentic AI penetration testing ARTEX in operations\" \u2014 the same framework CrowdStrike attributes to VAPOR PANDA. Microsoft also \"assesses that AI-enabled operations remain human-directed and have not become fully autonomous.\"",
   "Trend Micro researchers, writing on 28 October, reconstructed three exposed attacker environments aimed mostly at Japan. The one they rate as confirmed AI-driven execution of the full workflow used the open-source tool CyberStrikeAI, with a record reading \"credential-search agent 8/44\", and hit at least 19 identifiable organisations, at least 16 of them in Japan. They state \"we do not know whether the three cases share an operator\". Dream, in a report dated 26 October, says an operator with Claude Code and an AI-powered offensive platform called aiscan configured with DeepSeek and Kimi chained PHP2Shell and a Linux kernel bug so that \"the total chain \u2014 from unauthenticated HTTPS connection to persistent root webshell \u2014 completes in under 15 minutes\", bringing \"the total number of servers exploited to 25\" \u2014 24 rooted perimeter firewalls plus one internal management server \u2014 while adding that \"we have no direct evidence that the AI tools were specifically used to write the exploit\".",
   "AhnLab ASEC, writing on 27 October, published its own framework telemetry: it says it \"Tracked 600 IP addresses of ARTEX operational systems exposed to the internet; this number had increased to over 900 as of October 14\" and \"Secured 51 pieces of attack operation data\". On the South Korean financial-sector breaches it says that as of October 14 financial authorities had identified 16 affected financial institutions and that \"the confirmed scale of personal information leaks was approximately 100,000 records\", and that in Japan personal-information leak announcements via web systems \"totaled 119 as of October 6, 2026, already surpassing the 84 cases reported for all of 2025 and the 62 cases in 2024\". AhnLab adds that \"The extent to which tools like ARTEX were actually involved in incidents within the Korean financial sector is still under investigation.\"",
   "Caveats: every vendor count above is that vendor's own telemetry, and the two government documents attribute nothing new of their own \u2014 Canada's bulletin names the Storm-0940 cluster Microsoft attributed the week before, and Norway's assessment names no actor. ESET's APT Activity Report for April to September 2026, published 28 October, reaches the opposite conclusion about the aggregate picture: \"we have not noticed AI substantially reshaping the APT landscape over the past six months\", and it gives no count of how many groups or operations involved AI. GTIG notes the underlying tradecraft is unchanged \u2014 compromised credentials and unpatched systems \u2014 and that its recommended defences are \"long-standing security best practices\"."
