   }
  ],
  "bullets": [
   "Cursor said on 29 October that \"Between about 2:27am PT and 1:35pm PT today\" maliciously modified builds of Anthropic's Claude Code extension were available in its VS Code extension marketplace, and that they add an attacker-controlled SSH key, enable the local SSH service and open a reverse SSH tunnel. Cursor warned that \"Uninstalling the extension may not be sufficient \u2014 the payload drops standalone persistence scripts that keep running\", and said \"These malicious versions of the extension were not developed by Anthropic.\" Its notice, updated on 30 October \"to update the IOCs and clarify affected versions\", names \"Anthropic.claude-code version 2.1.312 and 2.1.313 for Windows and MacOS\", where early reporting had named only 2.1.313. The Hacker News reports Cursor as saying the incident was \"impacting at most 5,000 users\"; Cursor's notice as it now stands carries no affected-user count.",
   "BleepingComputer reported on 30 October that Cursor \"removed the malicious extension and temporarily shut down its entire marketplace to audit other extensions and rotate all access keys before making it available again\". It says researchers at Phorion and OpenSourceMalware found a malicious JavaScript loader inserted at the beginning of the extension's extension.js file, leaving the legitimate code otherwise unchanged, and that Phorion attributes the failure of remote access on many Windows and macOS systems to coding errors in the SSH setup routines \u2014 but that reconnaissance data was sent even when the backdoor failed, including username, hostname, public IP, Git email address and the names of environment variables associated with sensitive credentials.",
   "Microsoft published the loader analysis on 30 October. It says it \"confirmed that the malicious packages found on Cursor marketplace were never published nor distributed from Microsoft Visual Studio Marketplace and from npm @antropic-ai/claude-code\", that \"Anthropic confirmed to Microsoft that their systems were not compromised and they did not develop the malicious package posted in Cursor marketplace\", and that for anyone cleaning up, \"the loader may still write keys and persistence artifacts even when tunnel access fails\".",
   "Wiz, writing on 30 October, lists at least nine AI developer platforms disclosing incidents in October: Fireworks AI on Oct 6, Andromeda on Oct 9, Zeabur on Oct 14, Lightning AI on Oct 15, OpenCode on Oct 16\u201317, Railway on Oct 20, Warp on Oct 23, CommandCode on Oct 28 and Northflank on Oct 30. In two cases it investigated, stolen enterprise credentials drove approximately 800k Amazon Bedrock calls over thirty hours with about 90% succeeding, and approximately 1.7 million calls over thirteen hours with over a million succeeding, which Wiz says \"likely ran up over $100k in usage\" at list price.",
   "Symantec said on 29 October that an unauthorized relay service active since at least early 2026 \"has impacted systems at a dozen organizations, most of them in mainland China, as well as multinational subsidiaries based in South Korea, Taiwan, and Malaysia\", and works by rewriting the coding agents' own configuration files \u2014 pointing the API base URL at a relay-controlled domain, swapping in relay-issued credentials and setting the Codex sandbox to full access with network access enabled. Symantec adds: \"There is no evidence so far that the relay operator has abused this level of access.\" The Register reported on 29 October that Forever Security found embedded-browser flaws in Cursor, GitHub Copilot, Claude Desktop, VS Code and Codex that \"led to code execution in Cursor, data theft in Claude Desktop, Codex, and VS Code, and address bar spoofing in GitHub Copilot\", with the Codex and VS Code reports still open.",
   "Chainalysis posted a notice on 30 October saying \"This month, Chainalysis detected an external actor using offensive agentic techniques to gain unauthorized access to Chainalysis systems and internal tools\", with user IDs, full names, email addresses and organization role labels accessed, and no indication of access to investigation content, query results, clear-text passwords, MFA secrets or session tokens.",
   "Caveats: the 5,000 figure is Cursor's as relayed by The Hacker News, and Microsoft says the number of affected users is Cursor's rather than its own. Wiz says it is \"not able to definitively establish the relationship\" between the resellers and the initial compromise of the nine platforms. Chainalysis has published no technical detail \u2014 not which vulnerabilities were used, how long access lasted, how many customers were affected, or what role agents played. Symantec has not seen the relay access abused, and the vendors' own accounts are largely absent from the Forever Security disclosures."
